DAY 11 – FOR LOOP

1. WHAT IS A LOOP

A loop is used to execute a block of code repeatedly.

Advantages of loops:

• Reduces code repetition
• Saves development time
• Makes code shorter
• Improves code readability
• Makes repetitive tasks easier to automate


2. TYPES OF LOOPS IN PYTHON

Python mainly provides two types of loops:

• for loop
• while loop

In this lesson, the focus is on the for loop.


3. FOR LOOP

The for loop is used to iterate over a sequence or iterable.

Examples of iterables include:

• List
• Tuple
• String
• Set
• Dictionary
• Range

Syntax:

for variable in iterable:
    statements


4. HOW FOR LOOP WORKS INTERNALLY

A for loop internally works with an iterator.

The general process is:

Iterable
↓
iter()
↓
Iterator
↓
next()
↓
Next value
↓
Repeat until StopIteration

The for loop automatically performs this process.


5. ITER() FUNCTION

The iter() function is used to obtain an iterator from an iterable.

Iterable → Object that can be iterated over.

Iterator → Object that produces values one at a time.


6. NEXT() FUNCTION

The next() function gets the next value from an iterator.

When the iterator has no more values, it raises StopIteration.


7. RANGE() FUNCTION

The range() function creates a range object representing
a sequence of numbers.

The stop value in range() is excluded.

For example, a range from 1 to 5 produces values from 1 to 4.


8. REVERSE LOOP

A for loop can be used to iterate through values
in reverse order.


9. IF INSIDE A FOR LOOP

An if statement can be used inside a for loop.

for → Controls repetition

if → Controls selection

The condition is checked for every iteration.


10. EVEN NUMBERS USING FOR LOOP

A for loop can be combined with an if condition
to check whether each number is even.

The condition for an even number is:

number % 2 == 0


